{"body":"[hermit2, timerfd-recovery-opus-v2, opus, devbig014, role=impl]\n\nOwner: claude-coord-mega-lander (claude, devbig014) since 2026-09-28T15:23Z\n\n## Plain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nA timerfd was the one timer family Hermit still left to the host kernel.\n`timer_create`, `setitimer`, `alarm`, futex timeouts and epoll timeouts all fire\nfrom Hermit's logical clock. `timerfd_settime`, by contrast, armed a real host\ntimer. That timer expired after host milliseconds while the guest advanced by\nscheduler turns, so whether a timerfd had fired when another thread polled\ndepended on host timing. On the ptrace backend,\n`backend-parity-c/timer-family-identity` printed the timerfd line before the\nfutex and `timer_create` lines in two of four runs, and after them in the other\ntwo.\n\nThis moves Hermit toward faithful deterministic execution of real programs:\nevent loops built on timerfd (systemd-style services, libuv/tokio-style\nruntimes and many daemons) now get repeatable timer ordering under strict mode.\n\n- **Before:** the timerfd firing order varied from run to run.\n- **After:** the timer-family-identity guest's order matches Linux in every\n  run that produced a result (30 of 30 harness verify runs matched, 0\n  diverged; 20 of 20 strict reproducer runs with identical stdout).\n- **Full validation gains one cell,** `backend-parity-c/timerfd-semantics`\n  (42 timerfd behaviours compared with native Linux). `timer-family-identity`\n  stays unselected with a measured reason: 6 of its 36 harness runs ended in\n  a host skid-overshoot infrastructure error, and one such error fails the\n  whole validation node (see Validation).\n\nRecovered from rescue commit `851ec972c309c00b1b1b17f4ad96cd784e6ab1d6`. The\nfour findings from its review are fixed:\n\n- **Realtime ABSTIME:** there is no bogus clock conversion.\n- **select count:** select returns the correct count and remaining time.\n- **Invalid timespec:** it gets EINVAL and changes nothing.\n- **Lifecycle cleanup:** timer state lives on the open file description and\n  dies with it. The scheduler holds no timerfd entries, so there is nothing to\n  clean up on close, exit or failed exec.\n\n## Summary\n\nIn sequentialized runs that are not record/replay\n(`virtual_timerfds() = sequentialize_threads && !recordreplay_modes`, which is\nthe strict determinism contract), a timerfd is now virtual:\n\n- **State** (`detcore/src/fd.rs`):\n  - `TimerFdState` lives on the open file description, so `dup` and `fork`\n    aliases share one timer, as on Linux.\n  - Its fields are the clock, deadline, interval, consumed count,\n    CANCEL_ON_SET, and an arming generation.\n  - Expirations are a pure function of logical time. The next-expiry\n    arithmetic saturates, like the rest of logical time.\n- **Host descriptor:** the host timerfd still exists, so fd numbering and\n  poll/epoll registration are unchanged, but it is never armed.\n- **Scheduler:** unchanged. `detcore/src/scheduler.rs`,\n  `scheduler/timed_waiters.rs`, `syscalls/threads.rs` and `tool_global.rs`\n  are byte-identical to base `8929eeb716`.\n- **`detcore/src/tool_local.rs`** gains one read-only helper,\n  `timerfds_below(nfds)`, which lists the open virtual timerfd descriptors\n  below `nfds` so select can find them. It changes no scheduling or event\n  flow.\n- **Syscalls** (`detcore/src/syscalls/files.rs`): virtual implementations of\n  `timerfd_create`, `timerfd_settime` and `timerfd_gettime`. `read`, `readv`\n  and `preadv2(offset -1, flags 0)` on a timerfd go through the virtual\n  `read_timerfd`.\n- **Waits** (`detcore/src/syscalls/io.rs`):\n  - Blocking poll, ppoll, epoll_wait and epoll_pwait that can see a timerfd go\n    through one loop, `wait_with_timerfds`.\n  - Each retry probes the host with a zero timeout, then rescans the virtual\n    timer set, so a timer armed or re-armed by another thread mid-wait is seen\n    on the next retry.\n  - The loop returns when the merged count is nonzero, when the host probe\n    did not block, or at the guest deadline measured in logical time.\n  - An epoll wait with a nonzero timeout always uses the loop, so an\n    `epoll_ctl` ADD of an armed timerfd during the wait is also seen.\n  - select/pselect6 rescan on each retry. A blocking call with nfds above\n    64, which previously went to the kernel whole, stays in Detcore's retry\n    loop up to FD_SETSIZE (1024) when its read set names a virtual timerfd.\n    Zero-timeout calls merge timerfds for any nfds.\n\nRecord/replay and non-sequentialized runs keep the previous host passthrough,\nunchanged. Their waits block in the host, where a timer that is never armed\nwould never wake them. poll and ppoll skip the pollfd scan entirely in those\nmodes.\n\nDesign as implemented: `ai_docs/timerfd-virtual-time-design-2026-09-25.md`.\n\nCommits:\n\n- `6733ab1ef1461593a2ed2339359adaf418a238f3`: the product change, fixture,\n  manifest entry, inventory entry and design document.\n- `d10491308db759e385814bec46b9d56d78131fca`: selected both timerfd ptrace\n  verify cells in full validation (856 to 858 cells). Commit `7546968ea5`\n  below takes one of them back out.\n- `abcebadd407314b102d981983b2c0b665fda0005`: comment-only; names this PR in\n  the `TODO-HUMAN-REVIEW` markers.\n- `dbf27d2618fbfefb4f0061464f07baee3a1de376`: review round 1 (at\n  `abcebadd40`). Deleted the scheduler bookkeeping and its 5 unit tests\n  (Detcore unit tests 760 to 755).\n- `ec9fdf63a0dbfeecadc25dd6de331bae39221887`: review round 2 (at\n  `dbf27d2618`).\n- `7546968ea5cfa03a35be1927b22872eba3c58a32`: keeps `timer-family-identity`\n  out of full validation, with the skid measurement as its recorded reason\n  (858 back to 857 cells).\n- `9a60fca92a303ff6198b7e7539d6ed5dd3b7452c`: the round-2 test requests. It\n  adds fixture cases `huge_interval`, `max_interval` and\n  `epoll_pwait_masked_ready` (39 to 42), and the CI guard\n  `sigsuspend_with_armed_periodic_timerfd_reports_terminal_deadlock`.\n- `db90bf7603`: points the timerfd evidence constant at the 20 harness\n  runs made at `9a60fca92a`.\n\n### Review round 1 (at `abcebadd40`)\n\n- **Cross-thread arm during poll/epoll hangs or waits out the stale cap**\n  (blocker): fixed by the per-retry rescan. All variants return n=1 at\n  31 ms; native is 30 ms. At `abcebadd40` the results were 3003 ms, 5000 ms,\n  and hangs with timeout -1.\n- **Periodic timerfds as permanent scheduler events** (blocker): fixed by\n  deleting the scheduler bookkeeping. A 1 ms tick plus a 1 s nanosleep now\n  takes 1.5 s of wall time (was 12.1 s). A deadlock with a periodic timerfd\n  armed is reported (was a spin to the kill). This is now guarded in CI;\n  see Validation.\n- **Lifecycle keyed to the arming process** (major): moot, because there is\n  no per-process scheduler entry any more.\n- **readv reaches the host vessel** (major): fixed for readv and\n  `preadv2(-1, 0)`.\n- **create/gettime error order** (major): fixed.\n- **EFAULT before consuming** (minor), **poll scan outside virtual modes**\n  (nit): fixed.\n- **EPOLLET forward on gettime/settime**, **serde(skip) epoll link**:\n  disclosed as limits below.\n\n### Review round 2 (at `dbf27d2618`, both CHANGES-REQUESTED)\n\n- **Codex major: blocking select/pselect6 with nfds > 64 never sees a\n  timerfd.** A timerfd at fd 71 returned 0 after the timeout (native: 1 at\n  20 ms), and an infinite timeout would hang. Fixed as described above.\n  Above FD_SETSIZE the call returns ENOSYS instead of blocking. Fixture\n  cases `wide_select`, `wide_pselect` (infinite timeout) and\n  `wide_select_poll`.\n- **Codex major: readv with a faulting second iovec returned EFAULT.** Linux\n  returns the prefix length. Fixed; fixture cases `readv_partial` and\n  `readv_fault`.\n- **Claude major: `next_expiry` overflowed and aborted Hermit** (interval\n  17000000000 s: exit 125, `attempt to add with overflow`). Fixed with\n  saturating arithmetic. Unit test, plus fixture cases `huge_interval`\n  (17000000000 s) and `max_interval` (LONG_MAX s).\n- **Claude minor: settime saturated at u64::MAX, not KTIME_MAX.** Fixed;\n  values at or above 9223372036 s clamp to 9223372036 s 854775807 ns, as\n  `timespec64_to_ktime` does. The same two fixture cases check the value\n  read back.\n- **Claude minor: masked epoll_pwait returned ENOSYS even with a host fd\n  ready.** Fixed: it returns what is ready at entry and refuses only when it\n  would block. Fixture case `epoll_pwait_masked_ready` (a ready pipe beside\n  a timerfd 100 s out, and an expired timerfd).\n- **Claude minor: `timerfd_settime` on a timerfd received over SCM_RIGHTS\n  fails.** NOT fixed; disclosed below as a regression against base, for\n  reviewer acceptance. Forwarding settime to the host was tried: it arms the\n  host vessel on host time, while the sender's descriptor for the same file\n  reads the unarmed virtual state. That is a determinism leak, so the call\n  is refused.\n- **Claude nit: no CI guard for the deadlock report with an armed periodic\n  timerfd.** Added; see Validation.\n- **Codex nit: stale lit-test comment.** Fixed.\n\n## Determinism\n\nThe invariant is that every guest-visible timerfd value is a function of the\nlogical clock and the ordered syscall sequence. That covers expiration counts,\nreadiness, `gettime` remaining time, and epoll edge and oneshot state. No host\ntimer is ever armed.\n\nInformal proof:\n\n1. **Logical time.** It advances only at scheduler decisions, which Detcore\n   already makes deterministically.\n2. **Expiration counts.**\n   - `expirations(now)` is 0 before the deadline, and otherwise\n     `1 + (now - deadline) / interval` (1 for one-shot).\n   - `pending = expirations - consumed`.\n   - Both are pure integer functions of logical time and of the state last\n     written by a deterministic syscall. Inputs clamp at KTIME_MAX and the\n     next-expiry sum saturates, so no input reaches undefined arithmetic.\n3. **Readiness.** It is `pending > 0`, evaluated inside a syscall handler at a\n   deterministic logical instant.\n4. **Blocking waits.**\n   - Each retry of `wait_with_timerfds`, and of the select loops, is one\n     deterministic scheduler turn of the existing internal-polling path: a\n     zero-timeout host probe, then a rescan of virtual state at the thread's\n     logical time.\n   - The return decision uses only the merged count, whether the probe\n     blocked, and the logical-time deadline.\n   - A poll or ppoll that watches no virtual timerfd takes the old path and\n     makes no extra clock observation, so the event streams of unrelated\n     programs are unchanged. An epoll wait with a nonzero timeout always\n     takes the loop, because a timerfd may be added while it waits.\n   - Which select bitmap bytes are read depends only on the guest's\n     descriptor table, which is itself deterministic.\n5. **No aliasing.** Epoll interests are keyed by (fd, open file) with a weak\n   link to the file, so `dup`, `fork` and close/reopen cannot alias two\n   timers or leak a stale interest.\n6. **No scheduler coupling.** No timerfd state reaches the scheduler, so\n   timerfds cannot change fast-forward targets, same-instant event order or\n   deadlock classification. A timerfd waiter is an internal-polling thread\n   that stays in the run queue, as a plain poll waiter already does.\n7. **One clock.** Detcore has ONE logical clock: `clock_gettime` ignores the\n   clock id, and `clock_settime`/`settimeofday` are refused. So an ABSTIME\n   deadline needs no conversion for REALTIME, MONOTONIC or BOOTTIME, and\n   ECANCELED is unreachable.\n8. **Refusals are deterministic.** Every ENOSYS or EBADF listed under the\n   limits depends only on the syscall's arguments and the Detcore\n   descriptor table, never on host timing.\n\n## Linux Semantics\n\nBehaviour follows `fs/timerfd.c`. Each point below is checked against native\nLinux by `tests/backend-parity/fixtures/timerfd_semantics.c`, which prints 42\n`ok` lines natively (5 of 5 runs, identical output) and under Hermit. The\nrescue commit failed 12 of the fixture's original 19 cases.\n\n- **`timerfd_create`**\n  - Unknown flags return EINVAL, checked before the clock.\n  - REALTIME, MONOTONIC and BOOTTIME are accepted.\n  - The alarm clocks return EPERM, because they need CAP_WAKE_ALARM.\n  - Any other clock returns EINVAL.\n  - In host-passthrough modes the kernel validates, as before.\n- **`timerfd_settime`**\n  - Errors are checked in kernel order:\n    1. EFAULT for `new_value`, even with a bad fd.\n    2. EINVAL for unknown flags, or for a timespec that is negative or has\n       nanoseconds >= 1e9.\n    3. EBADF, or EINVAL for a descriptor that is not a timerfd.\n  - An invalid request changes nothing.\n  - A value at or above 9223372036 s clamps to KTIME_MAX.\n  - Re-arming resets the pending count.\n  - The old value is copied out last.\n  - TFD_TIMER_CANCEL_ON_SET is recorded only for REALTIME+ABSTIME and is never\n    an error.\n- **`timerfd_gettime`**\n  - The descriptor is resolved first (EBADF, or EINVAL for a pipe), then the\n    output pointer (EFAULT).\n  - It returns zeros when the timer is disarmed or a one-shot has expired.\n    Otherwise it returns the time to the next expiry and the interval.\n- **`read`, `readv`, `preadv2(-1, 0)`**\n  - A total buffer shorter than 8 bytes gives EINVAL and consumes nothing.\n  - Otherwise the u64 count is consumed, then scattered across the iovecs.\n    A copy that faults part-way returns the bytes already copied. One that\n    copies nothing returns EFAULT. Either way the count is gone, so the next\n    nonblocking read gives EAGAIN, as on Linux.\n  - An empty nonblocking timerfd gives EAGAIN.\n  - A signal gives ERESTARTSYS, so SA_RESTART restarts the read.\n  - Positioned reads (`pread64`) give ESPIPE.\n- **epoll**\n  - Interests are keyed by (fd, open file) with a weak link, so an interest\n    dies with the file's last reference.\n  - Only EPOLLIN is reported.\n  - EPOLLET fires once per arming generation, meaning per settime or per\n    consuming read.\n  - EPOLLONESHOT stays disabled until MOD.\n  - Only events that fit in `maxevents` consume an edge or a oneshot.\n  - A timerfd armed by another thread, or added with `epoll_ctl` by another\n    thread, during a wait is reported.\n  - A masked `epoll_pwait` returns a ready host fd or timerfd at entry.\n- **poll/ppoll** merge POLLIN, including a timerfd armed by another thread\n  during the wait.\n- **select/pselect6** set ready bits and count them, for timerfds at fd 71\n  and in finite, infinite and zero timeouts. The remaining timeout is not\n  truncated to the timer deadline.\n\nThe fixture also runs 1 µs periodic timers across a 100 ms sleep (at least 1000\nexpirations), with the timer both kept open and closed while armed.\n\n**Fixture correction, disclosed:** the rescue commit's `abstime_past` case\nread immediately after arming a past ABSTIME deadline. It failed natively in 8\nof 8 runs, because Linux expires a past deadline from its timer interrupt and\nnot inside `settime`. It now polls, with a 5000 ms timeout, before reading. The\nassertion itself (one expiration) is unchanged.\n\nDisclosed limits, which are not claimed as supported:\n\n- **SCM_RIGHTS (a regression against base for this case).** A timerfd\n  received over SCM_RIGHTS is not tracked by Detcore. `timerfd_settime` and\n  `timerfd_gettime` on it return EBADF; base forwarded them to the host and\n  succeeded. `read` on it was already EBADF at base. Forwarding would arm\n  the host vessel on host time, unseen by the sender's descriptor for the\n  same file.\n- `epoll_pwait` with a signal mask that must block, and `epoll_pwait2` on an\n  instance that watches a virtual timerfd, return ENOSYS rather than give up\n  the mask's atomicity. The equivalent `ppoll` case was already ENOSYS.\n- A blocking select or pselect6 with nfds above FD_SETSIZE (1024) whose read\n  set names a virtual timerfd returns ENOSYS. Detcore's scratch sets hold\n  1024 bits, and the kernel would block on the never-armed vessel.\n- A blocking select or pselect6 with nfds between 65 and 1024 that names a\n  virtual timerfd copies all three sets for nfds bits. Linux copies only up\n  to the process's fd-table size, so a guest whose set buffers are shorter\n  than nfds bits can see EFAULT where Linux would not.\n- Nested epoll does not merge timerfd readiness through the inner instance.\n- Timer events are appended after host events.\n- **Long waits cost polling rounds.** A long blocking timerfd wait advances\n  at the internal polling rate rather than jumping to the deadline, as plain\n  poll and epoll timeouts already do. Wall time for a 120 s guest wait, one\n  run each, debug build, strict, ptrace:\n  - timerfd read 11.0 s;\n  - timerfd poll 31.3 s, plain poll 34.7 s;\n  - timerfd epoll 28.7 s, plain epoll 27.8 s.\n\n  A one-hour wait of either kind does not finish within 30 s, the same as\n  before this PR.\n- `timerfd_gettime` and the settime old-value path forward a periodic timer on\n  Linux, which raises a fresh EPOLLET edge. The model raises edges only on\n  settime and consuming reads, so an EPOLLET waiter can miss that one extra\n  wakeup.\n- `preadv2` with offset -1 and nonzero flags, and reads through io_uring, AIO\n  or splice, reach the never-armed host timerfd.\n- The epoll interest's link to its timerfd file is `#[serde(skip)]`. Only the\n  ptrace backend, which keeps thread state in process, was measured.\n- BOOTTIME does not model suspend.\n\n## Validation\n\nHost: devbig014 (x86_64, AMD EPYC 9D85). Head `db90bf7603c7c142a8a4a6f1669319fbbb9ee21e`, base\n`8929eeb7162d2d86b1a1172c9ceaf2cc7d74b0ab` (current `main`, so no rebase).\nAll runs: debug build, ptrace backend, no relaxations.\n\n**Lint and unit tests (L0)**\n- `cargo fmt --all -- --check`: rc 0.\n- `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings`: rc 0 at\n  `ec9fdf63a0`, the last product-code change. Later commits touch only\n  tests and CI data; clippy on `-p hermit -p hermit-manifest-plan\n  --all-targets` is rc 0 at `9a60fca92a`.\n- `cargo test -p hermit-detcore --lib`: 755 passed, 0 failed.\n- `cargo test -p hermit-manifest-plan`: 434 passed (320 + 114).\n- `cargo test -p hermit --test signal_determinism -- sigsuspend`: 6 passed.\n\n**L4-style repeat of the selected cell (strict, default log level, info\nscope)**\n- Command: `target/debug/test-harness run --test backend-parity-c/timerfd-semantics --mode verify --backend ptrace`.\n- At `9a60fca92a`: 20 of 20 consecutive runs passed on the first attempt (host load average 31-48). Every report had\n  `bitwise_parity: true`, `verdict: matched`, comparator `BitwiseInfoV1`,\n  and 2915/2915 compared INFO messages (2808 at `ec9fdf63a0`, 2542 at\n  `dbf27d2618`; the extra messages come from the added cases).\n- At `ec9fdf63a0`: 20 of 20 matched, same settings.\n\n**`timer-family-identity` and the skid decision**\n- Harness verify, same command, at `dbf27d2618` and `ec9fdf63a0`: 36 runs.\n  30 matched with `bitwise_parity: true` and 0 diverged. 6 ended in\n  `HERMIT_SKID_OVERSHOOT`, which the runner classifies as outcome ERROR,\n  error kind `infrastructure`, with no verdict. Host load average during\n  those runs was 57 to 150.\n- Three neighbouring cells run alongside it: 0 skid errors in 48 runs.\n- The fixture spins 40 million iterations under `ITIMER_VIRTUAL`, unchanged\n  by this PR, which gives the preemption counter many chances to overshoot.\n  Validation does not retry that error, and one fails the whole node, so\n  selecting the cell would turn about one validation in six red for a host\n  reason. It stays `ci: false` with that reason recorded in the manifest.\n  The ordering fix is still evidenced: 20 of 20 strict reproducer runs at\n  `dbf27d2618` gave identical stdout, and 30 of 30 harness runs that\n  produced a verdict matched.\n\n**Deadlock guard (the round-2 nit)**\n- New test `sigsuspend_with_armed_periodic_timerfd_reports_terminal_deadlock`\n  in `hermit-cli/tests/signal_determinism.rs`, run by the\n  `test.hermit_integration` node (nextest count 160 to 161). It runs a guest\n  that arms a 1 ms periodic timerfd and then waits in `sigsuspend` with\n  every signal blocked, 3 times, and requires exit 125 with the deadlock\n  report and identical diagnostics each time.\n- It catches the defect. The same guest and flags through `bin/safehermit`\n  (`--log=off`, `--strict`, `--no-virtualize-cpuid`,\n  `--max-timeslice=disabled`, `--base-env=minimal`):\n  - Hermit built at `abcebadd40`, which still registered timerfds as\n    scheduler events: ran to the 20 s bound, rc 124, no report;\n  - this head: rc 125 with the report in 1.25 s;\n  - the no-signal control reported in both, in about 1.1 s.\n\n**Manifest, plan and audits**\n- `generate-validation-dag --check`, `expected-plan` (byte-identical to\n  `ci/expected-e2e-plan.json`) and `ci/compat-envelope/scorecard.rs check`:\n  clean.\n- Against base the plan adds exactly one cell,\n  `backend-parity-c/timerfd-semantics`: full validation 856 to 857 cells,\n  hosted-portable 852 to 853, scorecard selected comparable cells 853 to\n  854. No cell is removed, and no selector, comparison policy or timeout\n  changes.\n- `test-harness validate`: running at head `db90bf7603` since 2026-09-26T03:43:20Z; its outcome will replace this line and be posted as a comment.\n\n**Not established**\n- DBT, KVM, SaBRe and LiteInst: no claim. Only the ptrace cells are enabled\n  and measured.\n- Record/replay: unchanged, host passthrough.\n- The full `validate` run for this head had not finished when this was written.\n\n## Human Review Required\n\n- **Trigger 1: new syscall support.** Virtual `timerfd_create`,\n  `timerfd_settime`, `timerfd_gettime` and timerfd `read`/`readv`, plus\n  timerfd readiness in epoll, poll and select. `// AUTONOMOUS-BOT-IMPLEMENTED`\n  marks the dispatch entries, and `// TODO-HUMAN-REVIEW(PR-3229)` marks the\n  determinization blocks.\n- **Trigger 3: new determinization strategy.** Timerfd expiry is computed from\n  the logical clock on the open file description. Wait loops rescan virtual\n  readiness after every zero-timeout host probe, rather than blocking on a\n  host timer.\n- **Review focus:**\n  - the per-retry rescan in `wait_with_timerfds`, and routing every\n    nonzero-timeout epoll wait through it;\n  - the wide select path and its byte-granular bitmap reads;\n  - the ENOSYS choices (masked `epoll_pwait`/`epoll_pwait2`, select above\n    FD_SETSIZE) and the SCM_RIGHTS EBADF regression;\n  - the mode gate that leaves record/replay on host passthrough.\n\n","createdAt":"2026-09-26T01:05:11Z","number":3229,"state":"OPEN","title":"Drive timerfd readiness from the virtual clock"}
